   1: # iolbox runtime
   2: 
   3: The **runtime** is the small Linux environment that actually executes IOL and
   4: VPCS. Everything here builds **one** Debian-slim (bookworm) rootfs and then
   5: packages that *same* rootfs six ways:
   6: 
   7: - a **WSL2 import tarball** (`iolbox-rootfs.tar`) for the `wsl2` provider
   8: - a **VMware Workstation/Player appliance** (`iolbox-appliance-*.vmx` +
   9:   `.vmdk`) for the `vmware` provider (the default on machines that already
  10:   run VMware â€” see `docs/providers.md`)
  11: - an **OVA** (`iolbox-appliance-*.ova`) â€” the same appliance as a single
  12:   standard-format file for ESXi/VirtualBox/generic OVF import
  13: - a **Proxmox LXC template** (`iolbox-ct-*.tar.zst`)
  14: - a **native systemd tarball** (`iolbox-server-*.tar.gz`) for "bring your own
  15:   Linux box"
  16: - a **QEMU disk** (`iolbox-disk-*.qcow2`) for the bundled Windows launcher
  17:   (`tools/iolab-launcher`, the `qemu` provider's zero-prerequisite fallback)
  18: 
  19: Every target runs the identical rootfs, so a bug fixed once is fixed for
  20: all six â€” see `runtime/build-all-targets.sh`, which wraps the whole pipeline
  21: end to end.
  22: 
  23: This directory is **build tooling only**. It produces artifacts under
  24: `runtime/build/` (git-ignored) that get attached to GitHub releases. Nothing
  25: here bakes in Cisco software â€” the rootfs is empty of images; users supply
  26: their own `.bin`/`.iol` at runtime via the GUI's image upload.
  27: 
  28: The configuration baked in here mirrors the **proven reference runtime**
  29: (the hand-built `iolbox-rt` Ubuntu VM every product feature was validated
  30: on): same supervisor flags, same `/etc/hosts` hostname line, same stale-
  31: device cleanup, same two-NIC (host-only + NAT) shape.
  32: 
  33: ## Why these scripts don't run on this (Windows) machine
  34: 
  35: They are authored on Windows but are **Linux build scripts** â€” they shell out
  36: to `debootstrap`/`mmdebstrap`, `chroot`, `qemu-img`, and friends. Run them on:
  37: 
  38: - a real Linux box or VM (the iolbox-rt reference VM works: ~5 GB free disk
  39:   needed),
  40: - CI (GitHub Actions `ubuntu-latest`, which can apt-install everything).
  41: 
  42: Builder prerequisites (Debian/Ubuntu):
  43: 
  44: ```sh
  45: sudo apt-get install debootstrap qemu-utils parted e2fsprogs rsync \
  46:                      build-essential git
  47: ```
  48: 
  49: ## Layout
  50: 
  51: ```
  52: runtime/
  53:   README.md               this file
  54:   REDEPLOY.md              per-target redeploy/upgrade steps for a built artifact
  55:   qemu-compat.md            qemu-system-x86_64 (TCG) fallback notes
  56:   resources.env             single source of truth for vCPU/RAM across every target
  57:   build-rootfs.sh           debootstrap the base rootfs -> runtime/build/rootfs/
  58:   fetch-vpcs.sh             clone+build GNS3 vpcs (static link) -> vpcs binary
  59:   lib-disk.sh               shared disk-build code (partition/loop/mkfs/chroot/grub)
  60:                              consumed by pack-vmware.sh, pack-ova.sh, pack-qemu.sh
  61:   pack-wsl.sh               rootfs -> iolbox-rootfs.tar (wsl --import format)
  62:   pack-vmware.sh            rootfs -> bootable GPT disk -> vmdk + templated .vmx
  63:   pack-ova.sh               rootfs -> bootable GPT disk -> streamOptimized OVA
  64:   pack-lxc.sh               rootfs -> Proxmox LXC template tarball (.tar.zst)
  65:   pack-native.sh            supervisor+vpcs+files/native -> systemd install tarball
  66:   pack-qemu.sh              rootfs -> compressed qcow2 (--no-vmtools --no-nic-configs)
  67:   build-all.sh              orchestrator: rootfs -> [pack-wsl, pack-vmware] (legacy;
  68:                              prefer build-all-targets.sh for a full release build)
  69:   build-all-targets.sh      orchestrator: repo-root build-release.sh -> build-all.sh
  70:                              -> pack-ova/pack-lxc/pack-native/pack-qemu, all 6 targets
  71:   files/
  72:     iolbox-supervisor.service      systemd unit, autostarts the supervisor
  73:     iolbox-firstboot-iourc.service systemd oneshot, runs before the above
  74:     iolbox-dropbear.service        minimal SSH server (redeploy/debug access)
  75:     dropbear-keygen.sh             host-key generation for the above
  76:     prestart-clean.sh             ExecStartPre: stale tap devices + run-dir sweep
  77:     firstboot-iourc.sh            generates iourc from the runtime's own hostid
  78:     iolbox-init.sh                 non-systemd fallback init script
  79:     wsl.conf                      /etc/wsl.conf for the WSL2 import (pins hostname!)
  80:     80-ethernet-dhcp.network      networkd fallback: plain DHCP on en*
  81:     99-iolbox.conf                 sysctl drop-in (ip_forward off until NAT node starts)
  82:     iolbox-appliance.vmx.tmpl      templated VMware VMX (4 vCPU / 4 GB / 2 NICs)
  83:     console/                       pre-login console-banner unit (GUI URL + status)
  84:     lxc/                           Proxmox-specific units + pct-create.md recipe
  85:     native/                        systemd install/uninstall scripts for pack-native.sh
  86:     ova/                           templated OVF descriptor for pack-ova.sh
  87:     tools/                         tool-node packs (aaa/webserver/pc/...) baked into rootfs
  88:   build/                   OUTPUT (git-ignored)
  89: ```
  90: 
  91: ## The one rootfs, six packages
  92: 
  93: `build-rootfs.sh` is the only script that touches `debootstrap`/APT. It
  94: produces `runtime/build/rootfs/`, a directory tree that IS the runtime
  95: filesystem. Every `pack-*.sh` script downstream consumes that same
  96: directory:
  97: 
  98: - `pack-wsl.sh` just tars it (`iolbox-rootfs.tar`).
  99: - `pack-native.sh` doesn't touch the rootfs at all â€” it packages the
 100:   supervisor/vpcs binaries plus `files/native/` directly, since a native
 101:   install runs on the operator's own distro.
 102: - `pack-vmware.sh`, `pack-ova.sh`, and `pack-qemu.sh` all copy the rootfs
 103:   onto a disk image via the shared `lib-disk.sh` (partition â†’ loop â†’ mkfs â†’
 104:   rsync rootfs â†’ chroot kernel/GRUB â†’ per-target network configs), then
 105:   diverge only in the final container format (vmdk+vmx / streamOptimized
 106:   OVA / compressed qcow2) and a few profile flags (`pack-qemu.sh` always
 107:   builds with `--no-vmtools --no-nic-configs --zerofree`, since the QEMU
 108:   provider has no guest-IP discovery to power and gets its NIC MAC from
 109:   QEMU itself, not a baked networkd config).
 110: - `pack-lxc.sh` also skips the disk-image path â€” a Proxmox container shares
 111:   the host kernel, so it just tars the rootfs with LXC-specific unit
 112:   overrides from `files/lxc/`.
 113: 
 114: Rebuilding only one package does **not** require re-running
 115: `build-rootfs.sh` â€” just re-run the one pack script. The three disk-image
 116: scripts (`pack-vmware.sh`/`pack-ova.sh`/`pack-qemu.sh`) need root (loop
 117: devices, chroot, `grub-install`); `pack-wsl.sh`/`pack-lxc.sh`/`pack-native.sh`
 118: don't, but are harmless to run as root too.
 119: 
 120: ## Resource sizing (vCPU / RAM)
 121: 
 122: `runtime/resources.env` is the single place to change vCPU/RAM for every
 123: deployment target: `IOLBOX_VCPUS` (default 4) and `IOLBOX_RAM_MB` (default
 124: 4096). `pack-vmware.sh` and `pack-ova.sh` both source it at build time and
 125: substitute the values into the `.vmx` / OVF descriptor. The Proxmox LXC
