 109: 
 110: ## 3. WSL rootfs (WSL2)
 111: 
 112: **Artifact:** `iolbox-rootfs.tar`
 113: **Source:** `runtime/REDEPLOY.md`, `runtime/README.md`
 114: 
 115: Requires WSL2 (i.e. the Windows Hyper-V platform already enabled). Per
 116: `runtime/README.md`: **never enable Hyper-V on a machine you use for
 117: VMware Workstation** â€” the two are mutually exclusive platforms on the same
 118: box.
 119: 
 120: ```powershell
 121: wsl --import iolbox C:\Users\<you>\iolbox-wsl iolbox-rootfs.tar
 122: wsl -d iolbox -- systemctl status iolbox-supervisor.service
 123: # browse to http://localhost:4001
 124: ```
 125: 
 126: The imported distro autostarts the `iolbox-supervisor` systemd unit (and
 127: the `iolbox-firstboot-iourc` oneshot that mints the IOL license before it)
 128: on its own; the `systemctl status` check above is just a sanity check that
 129: it actually came up.
 130: 
 131: To redeploy/upgrade later, since a WSL rootfs has no in-place binary swap:
 132: 
 133: ```powershell
 134: wsl --unregister iolbox
 135: wsl --import iolbox C:\Users\<you>\iolbox-wsl <new-rootfs>.tar
 136: ```
 137: 
 138: **Sizing:** WSL2 uses its own global memory/CPU cap (`.wslconfig`), not
 139: `runtime/resources.env` â€” this target isn't one of the ones the resources
 140: file drives (see that file's comment listing which targets it covers).
 141: Adjust via `%UserProfile%\.wslconfig`.
 142: 
 143: **It worked if:** `http://localhost:4001` loads and `wsl -d iolbox --
 144: systemctl status iolbox-supervisor.service` shows active/running.
 145: 
 146: ---
 147: 
 148: ## 4. Proxmox LXC
 149: 
 150: **Artifact:** `iolbox-ct-v0.5.2.tar.zst`
 151: **Source:** `runtime/files/lxc/pct-create.md` (canonical â€” also shipped as
 152: `SETUP.md` inside the tarball itself)
 153: 
 154: ### 1. Upload the template
 155: 
 156: ```bash
 157: scp iolbox-ct-v0.5.2.tar.zst root@<pve-host>:/var/lib/vz/template/cache/
 158: ```
 159: 
 160: (Or Datacenter > Storage > Content > Upload; the storage must allow "CT
 161: Template".)
 162: 
 163: ### 2. Create the container
 164: 
 165: ```bash
 166: pct create <vmid> local:vztmpl/iolbox-ct-v0.5.2.tar.zst \
 167:     --unprivileged 1 \
 168:     --hostname iolbox \
 169:     --cores 4 \
 170:     --memory 4096 \
 171:     --swap 512 \
 172:     --net0 name=eth0,bridge=vmbr0,ip=dhcp \
 173:     --features nesting=0 \
 174:     --onboot 1
 175: ```
 176: 
 177: `--cores`/`--memory` mirror `runtime/resources.env`'s defaults (4 / 4096);
 178: resize live later with `pct set <vmid> --cores <n> --memory <mb>`, no
 179: rebuild needed.
 180: 
 181: **Important:** set `--hostname` at creation and never rename it after
 182: first boot â€” the IOL license (`iourc`) is minted from the container's
 183: hostid + hostname at first boot, and renaming later invalidates it.
 184: 
 185: ### 3. Add the TUN/TAP device lines the NAT node needs
 186: 
 187: Unprivileged CTs need explicit opt-in. Edit `/etc/pve/lxc/<vmid>.conf` on
 188: the Proxmox host:
 189: 
 190: ```bash
 191: cat >> /etc/pve/lxc/<vmid>.conf <<'EOF'
 192: lxc.cgroup2.devices.allow: c 10:200 rwm
 193: lxc.mount.entry: /dev/net/tun dev/net/tun none bind,create=file
 194: EOF
 195: ```
 196: 
 197: Restart if it was already running: `pct stop <vmid> && pct start <vmid>`.
 198: 
 199: ### 4. Start it and find the GUI
 200: 
 201: ```bash
 202: pct start <vmid>
 203: pct exec <vmid> -- systemctl status iolbox-supervisor.service
 204: pct exec <vmid> -- ip -4 addr show eth0
 205: ```
 206: 
 207: Browse to `http://<that-ip>:4001`.
 208: 
 209: **Security note (Proxmox-specific):** on a shared Proxmox host, `:4001`
 210: (no auth) is reachable by anything that can route to the CT's
 211: bridge/VLAN â€” a materially bigger exposure than a laptop-local appliance.
 212: Put it behind a firewall rule, a restricted VLAN, or a reverse proxy with
 213: auth; `pct set <vmid> --firewall 1` plus a CT firewall rule restricting
 214: `:4001` to your admin subnet is the simplest fix.
 215: 
 216: **It worked if:** `http://<ct-ip>:4001` loads and
 217: `pct exec <vmid> -- systemctl status iolbox-supervisor.service` is active.
 218: 
 219: ---
 220: 
 221: ## 5. Native (systemd Linux server)
 222: 
 223: **Artifact:** `iolbox-server-v0.5.2.tar.gz`
 224: **Source:** `runtime/files/native/README.txt`, `runtime/files/native/install.sh`
 225: 
 226: For "bring your own Linux box": bare metal, a cloud VM, or an existing
 227: on-prem hypervisor guest. Requires systemd, x86-64/glibc, and root.
 228: 
 229: ```bash
 230: tar xzf iolbox-server-v0.5.2.tar.gz
 231: cd iolbox-server-v0.5.2
 232: sudo ./install.sh                  # binds GUI/console/capture to 127.0.0.1 only
 233: # or:
 234: sudo ./install.sh --bind all       # binds 0.0.0.0 â€” LAN/VPN/tunnel reachable
 235: ```
 236: 
 237: `install.sh` installs to `/opt/iolbox` (binaries, `images/`, `labs/`,
 238: `run/`), installs and enables the `iolbox-supervisor` and
 239: `iolbox-firstboot-iourc` systemd units, writes `/etc/iolbox/bind.env`,
 240: generates the IOL license immediately (no reboot needed), starts the
 241: supervisor, and prints the GUI URL on completion.
 242: 
 243: Keep the hostname stable after install â€” the IOL license is keyed to this
 244: host's hostid + hostname; a cloud instance with cloud-init hostname
 245: randomization will silently break IOL licensing on the next reboot.
 246: `install.sh` warns if your hostname looks cloud-managed/ephemeral.
 247: 
 248: **Changing the bind mode later:**
 249: 
 250: ```bash
 251: sudo $EDITOR /etc/iolbox/bind.env      # edit IOLBOX_WS_ADDR / CONSOLE / CAPTURE
 252: sudo systemctl restart iolbox-supervisor.service
 253: ```
 254: 
 255: **Logs and status:**
 256: 
 257: ```bash
 258: journalctl -u iolbox-supervisor -f
 259: systemctl status iolbox-supervisor iolbox-firstboot-iourc
 260: ```
 261: 
 262: **Uninstall:**
 263: 
 264: ```bash
 265: sudo ./uninstall.sh              # prompts before deleting images/labs
 266: sudo ./uninstall.sh --yes        # non-interactive, deletes everything
 267: ```
 268: 
 269: **Sizing:** the native install doesn't source `runtime/resources.env`
 270: directly (it's a bare binary + systemd units, not a VM/container with a
 271: resource cap) â€” the host's own CPU/RAM is the ceiling; there's no
 272: target-specific vCPU/RAM knob documented for this artifact.
 273: 
 274: **It worked if:** with `--bind local` (default),
 275: `http://127.0.0.1:4001` loads from that same host, or via an SSH tunnel
 276: (`ssh -L 4001:127.0.0.1:4001 <user>@<host>`); with `--bind all`,
 277: `http://<host-ip>:4001` loads directly. `install.sh` prints the exact URL
 278: to use at the end of the run.
 279: 
 280: ---
